            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "This article reports a case of anaphylaxis that occurred in a 45-year-old man after he took Neptazol, a medication prescribed for hypertension. The patient, who had no previous drug allergies, experienced symptoms such as facial flushing, throat tightness, and difficulty breathing about 30 minutes after his initial dose of 20mg. He was treated in the emergency department with intramuscular epinephrine and recovered completely within a few hours. Following this incident, Neptazol was discontinued and not given again to the patient. \n\nThe case emphasizes the importance of recognizing and reporting first-dose reactions to Neptazol, particularly anaphylaxis, and highlights the need for healthcare providers to inform patients about early warning signs of this severe allergic reaction. This document is relevant to a patient-safety review because it addresses a serious adverse drug reaction and reinforces the critical nature of monitoring and responding to medication effects, especially when patients are starting a new medication.",
